Patch 50. After patch 49.

A watchdog that finds the guest stuck (Torchlight's, on a guest GPU wait that lasts more than
10 s) has nothing to say about where. rex::system::LogThreadDump(reason)
(include/rex/system/thread_dump.h) logs, as one error-level log call, every guest thread's
name, thread id, host thread id and state (running, waiting, suspended); for a waiting thread
the kernel objects it waits on (type and handle, the first 4 and the count), the kind of wait,
its timeout, how long it has waited and the guest LR of the kernel call; then the command
processor: ring read and write pointers, whether its worker runs, whether it is idle on an empty
ring or in WAIT_REG_MEM and for how long (with the register or memory address, function,
reference, mask and the last value read), and its pending calls.

It never waits on a lock, because the hang it is called for can be a lock held forever:
- Every XThread writes its own state into a fixed table (256 records, never freed): its ids,
  its current wait (XObject::Wait, WaitMultiple, SignalAndWait and XThread::Delay, which every
  Ke/Nt wait and blocking critical section goes through). Every field is an atomic, each part
  of a record has a sequence counter that is odd while it is written, and the dump keeps a read
  only when the counter was even and unchanged across it. A record being written at that moment
  is counted as "inconsistent".
- Other threads write a record's name (its own counter) and suspend count (one atomic), and
  only try its writer: on Windows a thread can be suspended in the middle of its own write, and
  the thread that resumes, renames or terminates it must not wait for it.
- The command processor is in the GPU plugin; it registers a section with the runtime
  (RegisterThreadDumpSection). Its worker writes its state into atomics; the dump only tries the
  pending calls' lock (PendingCalls::TrySize) and prints "inconsistent (locked)" when it is
  taken. Unregister waits until no dump runs the section, so the command processor can be
  destroyed after it.
- The one lock left is the log sink's, for the single call that writes the block.

Cost: each kernel wait on a guest thread stores a dozen atomics and reads the steady clock
once; the command processor stores a few relaxed atomics per packet loop, idle wait and
WAIT_REG_MEM wait.

Test: tests/unit/system/thread_dump_test.cpp. A thread's wait on two objects shows with its
kind, objects, timeout and LR, then as running once it ends; a record held by a writer on
another thread is counted as inconsistent and the dump still returns; renaming a record and
setting its suspend count return while its writer holds it; the command processor's section
prints a WAIT_REG_MEM wait and an idle one, and prints its pending calls as inconsistent while
another thread holds their lock; LogThreadDump returns with no kernel or GPU. Every dump in the
tests must return within 1 s. The tests built alone with ThreadSanitizer (clang 21, Linux):
three runs, no report. The SDK's tests pass with it (Linux: 1780); built for Android.

